{
  "summary": "The previous proposal's decomposition is directionally sound but underestimates three key risks. First, extracting modals/panes as 'purely presentational with minimal coupling' is misleading — several modals (AnswerQuestionsModal, UnifiedEditModal, RunScriptModal) receive FeatureFile objects or ScriptConfig lists and call back into PipelineApp via dismiss() results that trigger async workflows, so the extraction boundary isn't clean UI vs. logic but rather requires defining a stable callback/message protocol between the new modules and PipelineApp. Second, the proposed BoardDataStore with filesystem watches introduces platform-dependent behavior (inotify on Linux, kqueue on macOS, ReadDirectoryChangesW on Windows/WSL) and adds a new failure mode that's harder to debug than the current stat-based polling — given that the current _check_for_changes only stats files for a single active board (not all boards), the actual scalability concern is overstated for typical usage of <100 features per board; a simpler fix would be batching stats and short-circuiting on directory mtime before individual file stats. Third, the proposal doesn't address the hardest coupling: PipelineApp's 15+ action_* methods that interleave UI state updates (selected_feature, _plan_running flags, log output) with async pipeline orchestration (_run_plan_async, _run_implement_async, _run_ideating_async) — these 1,200+ lines are where the real god-class problem lives, and extracting them into a PipelineOrchestrator requires careful state synchronization since the workers mutate UI-visible state mid-execution via _log_line and status bar updates."
}
